  "id": 47,
  "added": "2026-10-22",
  "url": "https://youtu.be/ChDeUAJc9bE",
  "title": "Top Trainers Agree, These Are the Best Exercises for Building a Bigger Chest",
  "author": "Fit Media Channel",
  "category": "Health & Fitness",
  "core": "Fit Media's trainers rank seven chest exercises: (1) flat barbell bench press as the heavy compound foundation for progressive overload; (2) incline dumbbell press for the upper chest; (3) cable flies with constant tension, multiple angles and crossed hands; (4) machine press; (5) machine flies; (6) pullovers for the overhead stretch; (7) dips, leaning forward. Push-ups close the list as a finisher. Incline angles stress the upper chest, decline the lower chest.",
  "points": [
    "1. Flat barbell bench press: feet planted, grip slightly wider than shoulders, wrists over elbows. Lower with control, elbows angled (not flared), shoulder blades together, core tight. Do warm-up sets, then heavier sets with fewer reps. It's well supported (frame, spotter, help from front delts and triceps), so it's the key lift for progressive overload.",
    "Pec fibres fan out from under the shoulder. A flat bench loads them evenly. An incline (up to 45°) stresses the upper chest for fullness. A decline stresses the lower chest for the line under the pec.",
    "2. Incline dumbbell press: dumbbells allow a deeper stretch and a greater squeeze at the top, make the ~45° elbow angle easier, and allow variations (twists, close-grip supersets).",
    "3. Cable flies: flies isolate the pec (elbows locked, no help from other muscles). Dumbbell flies lose tension at the top. Cables give constant tension across the whole movement.",
    "Cable angles: high-to-low for the lower chest, chest height for the middle, low-to-high (also single-arm) for the upper chest. Vary angles between workouts. Tip: cross the handles in front to fully use the pec's job of bringing the arm across the body.",
    "4. Machine press: stable, easy to adjust, no spotter needed, lower injury risk, guided path that's good for beginners. Flat, incline and single-arm options. Use it later in the workout after free-weight compounds.",
    "5. Machine flies: constant tension. Vary body position (lean back for the lower chest, single-arm, bring the arm fully across). Cables stay first choice for flies.",
    "6. Pullover: stretches the pec overhead. Arms slightly bent, lower the weight behind the head under control. Done flat on chest day, or across the bench with hips dropped for maximum stretch. Often done on back day for the lats.",
    "7. Dips: lean forward (and use a wider grip) to shift work from the triceps to the pecs. Lower slowly. Add weight to progress, but don't go too deep, which can hurt the shoulders. Use them occasionally for variety.",
    "Push-ups: wide hands for chest, close for triceps, elbows at ~45°. Incline push-ups stress the lower chest, decline the upper chest. Use slow negatives and explosive ups, and rep to failure as a finisher."
  ]
